 "response": "**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component's ARIA structure (tablist/tab/tabpanel roles, `aria-selected`, `aria-controls`/`aria-labelledby` pairing, roving-tabindex attributes, native `hidden` panel-hiding) is well-formed, correctly wired, and \u2014 where the evidence pack speaks to it \u2014 confirmed correct at runtime. The design fails on exactly one axis, but that axis is load-bearing for the entire widget: `handleTabKeyDown` only recognizes `Enter` and `Space`. No `ArrowLeft`/`ArrowRight` handling exists anywhere in the component, so the roving-tabindex mechanism has no driver. A keyboard-only or screen-reader user who reaches this tablist can operate exactly one tab \u2014 whichever one currently holds `tabIndex={0}` \u2014 and has no way to reach the others. That is not a partial ARIA pattern; it is a complete keyboard-operability failure for a specific population, sitting inside an otherwise sound implementation.\n\n**Evidence Consulted (Phase 0)**:\n- Source: the full `TabsWidget` component and its CSS, read directly and in full (both inline in the material under review \u2014 no external files referenced or missing).\n- Test evidence: a CURATED evidence digest (an a11y-evidence-reader\u2013style product, not raw artifacts) reporting on `evals/results/keyboard-a11y-tester/driven/tabs-missing-arrow-nav.trace.json` (a keyboard-a11y-tester driven trace, 4 steps) plus a scoped read of 32 *other* components' finding files, with this component's own findings file explicitly excluded by the digest's stated pack policy.\n- Per this protocol's own Phase 0 rule for a11y-evidence-reader digests (\"a paraphrase, not the hard evidence itself... a finding that cannot be traced to a re-fetched handle is labeled digest-only\"), and because this review's operating constraints permit reading only the two supplied files (no re-fetch of the underlying `.trace.json` was possible), the trace-derived facts below are treated as **digest-only, corroborating evidence** \u2014 not independently re-verified. The finding they support does not depend on them: the source code alone (`handleTabKeyDown` having no arrow-key branch) is independently sufficient to establish the defect. The digest raises confidence from \"broken by code inspection\" to \"confirmed broken at runtime\" \u2014 nothing more, nothing less.\n- No axe-core scan, no `virtual-screen-reader` assertion output, and no real-keyboard `npx playwright test` transcript exist anywhere in the supplied material \u2014 the digest states this explicitly. This matters here specifically: every ARIA attribute in this component is syntactically valid, so an axe-core-style static scan would very likely find nothing wrong. This defect is exactly the class of \"80%-complete pattern\" gap this protocol exists to catch and that automated scanning misses.\n- The 32 other components' finding rows quoted in the digest (`sr-live-region-silent-desktop`, `positive-tabindex-desktop`, `sr-heading-skip-desktop`, `missing-accessible-name-desktop`, `no-skip-link-desktop`, `focus-appearance-weak-desktop`, etc.) belong to *other, unrelated components*. None of them is used as evidence for this component below \u2014 the digest itself is explicit that this component's own file was withheld, and attributing another component's finding to this one would be a fabrication.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**: Before reading the code closely, the expected failure modes for a hand-rolled Tabs widget were: (1) arrow-key navigation missing or incomplete, (2) roving tabindex present as markup but not actually driven by a key handler, (3) ambiguity between the manual- and automatic-activation models, (4) `aria-selected` not reliably reflecting state, (5) `aria-controls`/`aria-labelledby` pointing at the wrong or stale ids.\n\nFindings 1 and 2 were confirmed, and turned out to be the same defect: no handler exists to drive the roving tabindex, so it never roves past the initially-active tab. Finding 3 is confirmed in a specific sense: the code satisfies neither activation model, because the movement step both models depend on doesn't exist. Findings 4 and 5 were **not** confirmed \u2014 they were actively disconfirmed by both source inspection and the digest's trace excerpt (`aria-selected` and `aria-controls` are correct and, per the digest, DOM-verified live). The rest of the implementation \u2014 native `<button>` elements, correct `hidden` usage, visual+programmatic state pairing, no anti-pattern hits from the April 2026 known-defect list \u2014 was cleaner than the median prediction for this component type.\n\n**Critical Findings** (blocks access):\n\n1. **Roving-tabindex Tabs pattern has no keyboard driver \u2014 Left/Right Arrow do nothing, and no other tab is keyboard-reachable.** See `TabsWidget`, `handleTabKeyDown` (component source as provided, lines 17\u201322): the only branch is `e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' '`, which re-invokes `handleTabClick` on whichever tab is `e.currentTarget` \u2014 i.e., the tab that already has focus. There is no `ArrowLeft`/`ArrowRight` (or `Home`/`End`) branch anywhere in the component. Combined with `tabIndex={index === activeTab ? 0 : -1}` (line 34) correctly implementing roving tabindex *at the attribute level*, the practical effect is: exactly one `<button role=\"tab\">` is ever in the page's Tab sequence at a time (the active one), and nothing in the component can move focus, or trigger a panel switch, for any other tab via keyboard. Clicking is the only way to reach tabs 2..n.\n   - Evidence (digest-only, not independently re-fetched \u2014 see Evidence Consulted above): per the supplied digest of `tabs-missing-arrow-nav.trace.json`, `step_0002` (keystroke `ArrowRight`) and `step_0003` (keystroke `ArrowLeft`) both report `active_element_selector:\"#tab-overview\"` and `focus_moved:false`, while the two `Tab`-keystroke steps (`step_0001`, `step_0004`) both report `focus_moved:true`. The digest also reports the AX `states` dict for `#tab-overview` (role \"tab\", name \"Overview\", `selected:true`, `controls:\"panel-overview\"`) as byte-identical across `step_0001`\u2013`step_0003` \u2014 consistent with focus, and therefore state, never changing in response to either arrow key.\n   - User group impacted: keyboard-only users (motor/dexterity disabilities, switch-access and eye-tracking device users, and anyone who simply doesn't use a mouse) and screen reader users navigating with standard keyboard commands \u2014 which is the default interaction mode for a tablist under every major screen reader (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver all pass arrow keys through to the page for a native tablist; this isn't an AT-specific quirk, it's the universal expectation the APG pattern exists to satisfy).\n   - WCAG/APG: **WCAG 2.2 \u00a7 2.1.1 Keyboard (Level A)** \u2014 the function of switching to tab 2 or tab 3 has no keyboard operation. Per **WAI-ARIA APG Tabs Pattern**, \"Keyboard Interaction,\" Left/Right Arrow (for a horizontal tablist) must move focus among tabs; that requirement is unmet in full, not partially. Note: this is *not* a WCAG 2.1.2 No Keyboard Trap violation \u2014 focus is never stuck; `Tab` still moves forward and out of the widget (confirmed by `step_0004`). The bug is operability of a function, not escapability, and I want that distinction on the record rather than reflexively citing 2.1.2.\n   - Confidence: HIGH. The absence is provable from the source alone (no arrow-key branch exists in the only keydown handler in the file), independent of the digest; the digest corroborates it at runtime.\n   - Why this matters: a sighted mouse user experiences this component as three working tabs. A keyboard-only or screen-reader user experiences it as a single-tab widget that happens to announce \"position 1, set size 3\" \u2014 telling them content exists that they have no way to reach. That gap between what's announced and what's operable is a particularly bad failure mode: it's discoverable (the user knows they're missing something) but not solvable by the user.\n   - Fix: in `handleTabKeyDown`, add `ArrowRight`/`ArrowLeft` (and, for completeness, `Home`/`End`) branches that compute the next index (wrapping at the ends, per APG convention), call `.focus()` on the corresponding tab button (a ref or `document.getElementById`/`querySelector` on `tab-${tabs[nextIndex].id}` both work), and update `activeTab` \u2014 either immediately (automatic activation, matching the \"Arrow Left/Right should cycle through tabs\" language in this fixture's own Expected Behavior) or only on a subsequent `Enter`/`Space` (manual activation). Either is APG-conformant; what's not conformant is doing neither. Call `e.preventDefault()` on the arrow keys as well, to prevent any incidental scroll.\n\n   ```\n   ### A11y Evidence Finding\n   finding_id: tabs-missing-arrow-key-navigation\n   fingerprint: deadbeef00000001  (placeholder \u2014 no hashing utility was available under this task's constraints; regenerate via standard tooling before this finding enters any tracked corpus)\n   source: keyboard-a11y-tester driven trace, evals/results/keyboard-a11y-tester/driven/tabs-missing-arrow-nav.trace.json (via CURATED evidence digest \u2014 digest-only, not independently re-fetched by this reviewer); corroborated independently by direct inspection of `handleTabKeyDown` in the supplied component source\n   wcag_or_apg: WCAG 2.2 \u00a7 2.1.1 Keyboard (Level A); WAI-ARIA APG Tabs Pattern, Keyboard Interaction (Left/Right Arrow requirement)\n   section_508_fpc_context: not in scope (component-level review; no federal/508 scope declared in the review request)\n   severity: CRITICAL\n   perspective_alarms: screen_reader=HIGH, keyboard=HIGH, low_vision=MEDIUM, cognitive=MEDIUM, vestibular=LOW, auditory=LOW, environmental_contrast=MEDIUM\n   evidence: source \u2014 `handleTabKeyDown` (component source as provided, lines 17-22) branches only on Enter/Space, no Arrow/Home/End branch exists; digest \u2014 trace steps step_0002 (ArrowRight) and step_0003 (ArrowLeft) both report focus_moved:false and an unchanged AX-states dict for #tab-overview, vs. focus_moved:true on both Tab-keystroke steps (step_0001, step_0004)\n   reproduction_steps: Focus the active tab (e.g., via Tab). Press ArrowRight. Observe focus and aria-selected state on all tabs; per the source and the digest-reported trace, nothing changes. Repeat with ArrowLeft.\n   expected_behavior: Per WAI-ARIA APG Tabs Pattern, ArrowRight/ArrowLeft should move focus to the adjacent tab (wrapping at the ends), enabling the user to reach and (per this fixture's own stated Expected Behavior) activate any tab.\n   actual_behavior: Focus remains on the originally-focused tab; no AX state changes; no other tab is reachable via keyboard.\n   trend: new\n   ```\n\n**Major Findings** (significantly degrades experience): None identified. Every other ARIA attribute, state-communication path, and semantic-HTML decision in this component held up under review (see Multi-Perspective Notes and What's Missing below for the specific checks performed).\n\n**Minor Findings** (friction but workaround exists): None identified at MINOR severity \u2014 see Enhancements below for sub-CRITICAL observations, and Open Questions for items too speculative to score.\n\n**Enhancements** (best practice not met):\n- `Home`/`End` key support is also absent. Per WAI-ARIA APG, these are the *optional* enhancement on top of the *required* Left/Right behavior \u2014 on their own, their absence would be ENHANCEMENT-tier, not CRITICAL. Implement alongside the Left/Right fix above; it is effectively free once the focus-movement function exists.\n- `.tab-button` has `transition: color 0.2s` (CSS, line 87) with no `prefers-reduced-motion` guard. This is a trivial, non-positional color fade \u2014 it does not rise to a scored finding \u2014 but WCAG 2.3.3 (AAA) would prefer even this suppressed under reduced motion. Flagging for completeness, not as a blocker.\n- Tab panel content is a bare `<p>{tab.content}</p>` (line 54) with no heading. This is content-dependent (the fixture's content is a placeholder) rather than a code defect, so I'm not scoring it \u2014 but if real panel content is more than a paragraph, a heading at the top of each panel gives screen reader users a navigation anchor they currently don't have.\n\n**What's Missing** (gaps, unhandled edge cases, unstated assumptions):\n- This component's *own* keyboard-a11y-tester findings file was withheld from the evidence pack by stated pack policy. I have no way to confirm or rule out additional defects (e.g., this component's own focus-appearance measurement) that a dedicated crawl of this exact component might surface. The CRITICAL finding above does not depend on that file, but I want it on record that my evidentiary ceiling for *this specific component* is capped at the driven trace plus source inspection \u2014 not a full detector sweep.\n- No axe-core, `virtual-screen-reader`, or real-keyboard Playwright transcript exists for this component in the supplied material (the digest confirms this explicitly). None of those tiers would likely have caught the CRITICAL finding above anyway (see Evidence Consulted), which is itself worth noting: this is a defect class that specifically requires either a driven keyboard session or a human keyboard walkthrough to surface.\n- Disabled tabs are not modeled in this fixture (no `tab.disabled` handling visible). If the real usage of this component ever needs a disabled tab, `aria-disabled=\"true\"` plus removal from the roving-tabindex cycle would need explicit handling \u2014 not a finding against the code shown, just an edge case the current shape doesn't yet need to answer.\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes**:\n- **Screen reader user**: Structure is sound and, per the digest, confirmed live: on initial focus, the reported announcement is \"tab, Overview, selected, 1 control, position 1, set size 3\" \u2014 a complete, correct name/role/state/set-size announcement. But \"set size 3\" tells the user two more tabs exist, and the CRITICAL finding means they have no keyboard path to either one. A screen reader user hears evidence of content they cannot reach \u2014 arguably a worse experience than not knowing it exists at all.\n- **Keyboard-only user**: `Tab` correctly lands on the active tab and correctly leaves the widget going forward (no trap). `Enter`/`Space` correctly re-select the already-active tab (a harmless no-op). Arrow keys do nothing at all, per both the handler code and the digest's trace. There is no keyboard path to tabs 2 or 3. This is the same defect as above, restated from the perspective it hits hardest.\n- **Low vision user (200% zoom, high contrast)**: Focus indicator (`outline: 3px solid #0066cc; outline-offset: 2px`, lines 100\u2013103) is present on every tab button regardless of state \u2014 good, and it should scale correctly under browser zoom. By hand-calculation from the stated hex values (not tool-measured \u2014 no contrast tool was available under this review's constraints, and no `background-color` is specified on `.tabs-container` or its ancestors, so a white page background is assumed): `#666` text and `#0066cc` text/outline against white both land around 5.5\u20135.7:1, comfortably clearing the 4.5:1 text and 3:1 non-text thresholds *if the white-background assumption holds*. Separately: `[role=\"tablist\"]` is `display: flex` with no `flex-wrap` (lines 72\u201376); with enough tabs or long labels at high zoom this could overflow rather than wrap, but I can't confirm this with only 3 short-label tabs shown \u2014 flagged in Open Questions, not scored.\n- **Cognitive accessibility**: No errors, timeouts, or destructive actions apply here. The one relevant point: every tab-widget convention a user has encountered before (browser tabs, OS tabs, other web tab patterns) supports arrow-key cycling. This component silently breaks that learned convention for keyboard users specifically, which is a consistency violation on top of being an operability violation.\n- **Vestibular & motion sensitivity**: LOW alarm. The only motion-adjacent CSS is a 0.2s color transition on hover/active state (line 87) \u2014 no parallax, no auto-play, no positional animation. Not a meaningful vestibular trigger.\n- **Auditory access**: LOW alarm. No media elements in this component; not applicable.\n- **Environmental contrast**: MEDIUM alarm. Values pass by hand-calculation (see Low Vision above) but rest on an unverified background-color assumption and were not tool-measured \u2014 noting the gap rather than asserting a false level of precision.\n\n**Perspective Alarm Levels**:\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | HIGH | Confirmed: announcement is correct but 2 of 3 tabs are unreachable \u2014 AT users hear evidence of content they cannot operate |\n| Keyboard-only | HIGH | Confirmed by both code and digest-reported trace: Arrow keys are no-ops; only the active tab is ever in the Tab sequence |\n| Low vision | MEDIUM | Standard composite widget; unverified background assumption for contrast, unconfirmed tablist-overflow risk at high zoom |\n| Cognitive | MEDIUM | Standard widget; keyboard behavior breaks a near-universal user expectation for tab controls |\n| Vestibular & motion | LOW | Trivial, non-positional 0.2s color transition only |\n| Auditory access | LOW | No media elements present |\n| Environmental contrast | MEDIUM | Hand-calculated estimates pass but are unmeasured and background-dependent |\n\nScreen reader and Keyboard-only both land at HIGH \u2014 per this protocol, that's the signal to route this component to `/perspective-audit` for deeper review before it ships, on top of the fix below.\n\n**Verdict Justification**: One CRITICAL finding, independently established by source-code inspection and corroborated (digest-only, not independently re-fetched) by a driven keyboard trace: the Tabs widget has no arrow-key navigation, so keyboard-only and screen-reader users can reach exactly one of however many tabs exist. That alone is enough to block shipping \u2014 per this protocol's Realist Check, it survives all four calibration questions: the realistic worst case is a hard content-access wall, not a minor inconvenience; the affected population (keyboard-only and screen-reader users) is not a narrow edge case; automated tooling would not have caught this since every ARIA attribute is syntactically valid, so detection-in-production would be slow; and it constitutes complete access loss to a specific function for a specific population, which this protocol's recalibration rules say to never downgrade. Everything else reviewed \u2014 semantic HTML choices, ARIA attribute correctness and DOM-verified wiring, state communication for the selected state, and the full April-2026 known-anti-pattern checklist \u2014 came back clean, which is why this is REVISE and not REJECT: the fix is narrow, well-understood, and doesn't require rethinking the component's structure, only adding the one missing interaction handler. Upgrade path to ACCEPT: implement Left/Right (and ideally Home/End) arrow-key focus movement wired to the existing roving-tabindex state, verify with a re-run keyboard trace or `npx playwright test`, and confirm the HIGH-alarm screen reader/keyboard perspectives clear on re-review.\n\n**Open Questions (unscored)**:\n- Whether `[role=\"tablist\"] { display: flex; gap: 4px }` (no `flex-wrap`) causes real overflow at 200% zoom or in high-contrast/large-text configurations depends on tab count and label length not established by this 3-tab, short-label fixture. Flagging rather than scoring \u2014 a developer could reasonably say \"we never have enough tabs for this to matter\" and be right.\n- The color-contrast figures given above (~5.5\u20135.7:1) are hand-calculated from the stated hex values assuming a white page background, since `.tabs-container` and its ancestors specify no `background-color` and no contrast-measurement tool was available under this review's constraints. If the real deployment context has a non-white ancestor background, these numbers don't hold and would need re-checking with an actual contrast tool.\n- Whether this component's own (withheld) keyboard-a11y-tester findings file contains additional defects \u2014 e.g., its own focus-appearance measurement \u2014 is unknown; the digest explicitly excluded it by stated pack policy, and I have no basis to speculate either way.\n- Whether the eventual fix should implement automatic activation (arrow keys immediately switch panels) or manual activation (arrow keys move focus only, `Enter`/`Space` confirms) is a legitimate design choice either pattern satisfies APG; this fixture's own \"Expected Behavior\" language (\"Arrow Left/Right should cycle through tabs\") leans toward automatic activation, but I'm not scoring the choice itself, only the fact that neither is currently implemented.\n",
